WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.500
- [Kim] Hey this is Kim from Khan Academy,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.660
and today I'm learning about Article Five

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.160
of the U.S. Constitution,

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.750
which describes the
Constitution's amendment process.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.340
To learn more about Article Five,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.500
I talked to two experts,
Professor Michael Rappaport,

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.760
who is the Darling Foundation Professor

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.370
at the University of
San Diego School of Law,

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.070
where he also serves as
the Director of the Center

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.960
for the Study of
Constitutional Originalism.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.520
And Davis Strauss,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.150
who's the Gerald Ratner
Distinguished Service Professor

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.780
of Law at the University
of Chicago Law School,

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.040
and author of The Living Constitution.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.276
Professor Strauss, Article
Five provides this process

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:42.700
for amending the Constitution.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.980
Can you take us through
that process a little bit?

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:45.933
How does it work?

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.218
- [Strauss] A quick
description, the process is

00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:51.035
it's really hard, it's really hard

00:00:51.035 --> 00:00:53.410
to amend the Constitution.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.260
There are actually a couple
of different processes

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.101
that are laid out in Article Five,

00:00:59.101 --> 00:01:01.540
but only one has ever been used.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.497
An amendment starts in Congress,

00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:06.240
and two thirds of each House of Congress,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.460
two thirds of the House of Representatives

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.760
and two thirds of the Senate

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.950
has to approve the amendment.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.570
And then it goes to the States.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.625
And three quarters of the States

00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:18.210
have to approve the amendment.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.480
So you have to have
really strong consensus

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.590
in order to get the
constitution changed that way.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.220
- [Kim] So Professor Rappaport,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.490
take us through this process
of amending the Constitution.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.933
Why did the Framers set it up this way?

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:35.130
- [Rappaport] The Framers gave a good bit

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.120
of thought to coming up
with an Amendment process

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.222
because they recognized
that the Constitution

00:01:41.222 --> 00:01:43.340
might need to be changed over time,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.930
either because there were problems with it

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.030
that weren't anticipated,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.810
or because circumstances
or values changed.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.900
So there are two steps
to the amendment process.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.600
For an amendment to go
into the Constitution

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.440
to become part of the Constitution,

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.590
it has to be both proposed and ratified.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.590
On the proposal side,
the Congress can propose.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.800
Alternatively, a proposal can come from

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.510
the action of the state legislatures.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.810
So two thirds of the
state legislatures say,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.900
we'd like to have a
Constitutional Convention

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.680
propose an amendment.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.950
So there's two parts of that obviously.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.480
The state legislatures
have got to want it,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.410
and then you get the calling

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:27.870
of a Constitutional Convention.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.070
Okay, that's the proposal side.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.730
There's also the ratification side,

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.630
which is a little bit simpler.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.153
You need three quarters of the States

00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:39.270
to ratify a Constitutional amendment,

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.060
and they can ratify it either through

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.300
the actions of the state legislatures,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:48.870
or the actions of state conventions,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.210
which are special bodies
which would be elected

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.710
in order to decides one question,

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.440
whether or not to ratify

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.080
that proposed Constitutional amendment.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.090
- [Kim] This is fascinating.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.800
So I actually had no
idea about the two thirds

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.490
of the state legislatures being able

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.610
to propose a Constitutional amendment.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.340
How often does that happen?

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.210
- [Rappaport] It has never occurred

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.173
throughout our history,
although a couple of times

00:03:16.173 --> 00:03:19.160
there were actions taken to
sort of move in that direction,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.934
but we've never actually had
a Constitutional Convention

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:25.670
that has proposed any amendments.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.270
It's important to go into why the Framers

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.440
would've set up the
system the way they did.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.790
The most usual situation
is for the Congress

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.290
to propose the Amendments

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.710
and that's happened in
all of the 27 amendments

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:43.070
which have been ratified to
become part of our Constitution.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.930
But what happens if the
Congress is the problem?

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.830
What happens if the Congress is doing,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.280
is usurping power or they're standing in

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.860
the way of changes that are important,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.450
or they need to be reformed.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.500
You can't count on the Congress
to wanna reform itself.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.810
So what they did was to have
this alternative mechanism

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.047
which would bypass the Congress.

00:04:03.047 --> 00:04:04.840
And that alternative mechanism

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.160
was the Constitutional Convention.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.690
So the state legislatures
propose, apply for it,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.030
and then the separate entity,

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.800
the Constitutional
Convention, makes a proposal.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.010
So they were quite explicit
in discussing this,

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.177
that they wanted this as an
alternative to the Congress.

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:22.580
- [Kim] So was this on purpose,

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.658
that they made it very difficult
to amend the Constitution?

00:04:25.658 --> 00:04:27.450
- [Strauss] Well it sure seems like it.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.550
Now, of course we don't know back then

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.505
what they had in mind,
whether they thought, well,

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:35.070
the House, the Senate, the States,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.060
they'll sort of be all be run
by the same kind of people

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.110
and they'll kind of agree on things.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.110
Maybe they thought that.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.480
We just don't know.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.760
But whatever they were thinking,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.370
what they gave us was a
very difficult process

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.960
to get through.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.766
- [Kim] So how long was it from the period

00:04:50.766 --> 00:04:54.726
when the Constitution was first ratified

00:04:54.726 --> 00:04:58.551
to the First Amendment to the Constitution

00:04:58.551 --> 00:05:00.707
beyond the Bill of Rights?

00:05:00.707 --> 00:05:03.744
- [Rappaport] Okay, so
the first 10 amendments

00:05:03.744 --> 00:05:05.577
were ratified in 1791.

00:05:06.708 --> 00:05:11.708
And then just a mere three years later,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.037
we had the Eleventh Amendment.

00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:16.670
- [Strauss] It was the
Eleventh Amendment in 1798

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.790
to correct really kind
of a technical problem

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.910
that the Supreme Court did something

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.310
that the Framers really
didn't anticipate it would do,

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:26.280
didn't want it to do,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.800
and the Eleventh Amendment
was adopted to correct that.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.610
The Twelfth Amendment was adopted in 1804

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:38.320
after the really kind of a
disaster in the election of 1800,

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.560
when there was a tie in
the Electoral College.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.710
The Framers had not foreseen
the rise of political parties

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.460
and political parties made
the system for electing

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.190
the President they had given
us very difficult to work with.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.220
But then there was nothing.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.160
That was 1804.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.570
Then there was nothing
until after the Civil War.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.240
And after the Civil War,
there were three amendments,

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.490
then nothing again really
until the Progressive Era

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.230
in the early 20th Century,

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.210
when there were again
a bunch of amendments.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.400
And then after then, things
had sort of tailed off.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.400
So we really see these kind of, as I said,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.580
these kind of waves in our history.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.790
- [Kim] What do you think
brings those waves on?

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.490
Why are there some eras
when there are lots

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.680
of Constitutional
amendments and other eras

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.068
when there's nothing?

00:06:20.068 --> 00:06:22.370
- [Strauss] Well Kim, here
I'm gonna say something

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:24.150
that I think some people
will disagree with

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.840
but I think it's right and that is that,

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.300
I don't think the process
of amending the Constitution

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.470
has really been the way
we actually change it.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.240
I think what happens is just because

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.660
the amendment process is so difficult,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.899
we've worked out other
ways of changing things.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:44.510
And so amendments come along sometimes

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.648
because a change has already happened

00:06:46.648 --> 00:06:48.930
and the people decide, well
let's put it in the Constitution

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.720
just so we can kinda have
official recognition of it.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.480
But a lot of times changes happen

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.700
and they are a little
bit too controversial

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.530
to get into the Constitution,

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.240
but they seem pretty
solid and pretty secure

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.190
so we just don't, I
guess it's fair to say,

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.310
don't bother to amend the Constitution

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:07.790
or don't wanna go through the process

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:09.530
of amending the Constitution.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.882
- [Rappaport] Very often
people's values may change

00:07:12.882 --> 00:07:17.882
or they may differ from
what's in the Constitution

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.961
and it may take a time or
circumstances may finally

00:07:21.961 --> 00:07:26.447
occur that crystallize this desire

00:07:26.447 --> 00:07:28.980
to change the Constitution,

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.510
and all of a sudden the
opportunity is there

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:36.159
and people can suddenly pass
a Constitutional amendment.

00:07:36.159 --> 00:07:39.050
It's only gonna occur during
certain circumstances,

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.120
especially when there's
strong support for it.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.160
- [Kim] Very interesting, yeah.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.230
So it's unlikely that we're gonna have

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.540
a Constitutional amendment
any time in the near future.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.124
When was the last
Constitutional amendment?

00:07:51.124 --> 00:07:55.400
- [Rappaport] So the simple
answer to that was in 1971,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.800
we got the 26th Amendment,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.310
that was both proposed
and then remarkably,

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.291
it's an all time record,

00:08:03.291 --> 00:08:07.750
proposed and ratified in
three months and eight days.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.130
And that was the amendment that guaranteed

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.719
the right to vote of 18 year olds.

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:15.050
- [Kim] Ah, right.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.660
So it's sort of as a
response to the Vietnam War.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.080
- [Rappaport] Yes, yes.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.150
But there actually has been
one additional amendment,

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:24.820
the 27th Amendment, right?

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:27.180
So why isn't that the most recent one?

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:28.500
Well here's the funny thing about it.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.278
The 27th Amendment was proposed

00:08:31.278 --> 00:08:36.278
as part of the original
Bill of Rights in 1789.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.980
So this amendment was proposed in 1789,

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:43.890
ratified in 1992, so it took 202 years.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.210
- [Kim] Interesting.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.860
And what's the 27th Amendment about?

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.080
- [Strauss] That has to do

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.410
with Congressional salary increases.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:55.300
It basically says if Congress
wants to raise its own salary,

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:59.150
the increase can't take effect
until the next election.

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.380
So it basically gives the
voters a chance to say,

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.010
hey, we don't like what you did.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.713
We're gonna vote you out of office

00:09:05.713 --> 00:09:08.630
for increasing your salary.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:12.940
- [Kim] So one thing that
strikes me about Article Five

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.623
and just the fact that
the Founders included

00:09:15.623 --> 00:09:18.240
an amendment process altogether,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.126
it seems very humble and farsighted

00:09:21.126 --> 00:09:26.126
to include a way for the
document itself to evolve,

00:09:26.995 --> 00:09:28.260
in a way.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.070
Do you think that the Framers approached

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.850
the Constitution with the
idea that there were things

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:37.590
in the future that they just
wouldn't be able to anticipate?

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:39.350
- [Strauss] They had before them

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.010
and were acutely aware of the history

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.960
in which efforts to establish
governments had failed

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.900
and they were really
trying to work with that

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.690
and make sure they
didn't do the same thing.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.370
So they knew what a hard
job they were embarking on.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.760
And they made it clear,

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.440
there's a famous passage in
which James Madison said,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.930
look, we know a lot of these provisions

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.440
that we're writing in the Constitution,

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.220
their meaning is unclear

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.500
and their meaning will have to be,

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.100
his phrase was liquidated.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.500
Which is to say, people have to figure out

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.430
what this means because we
know what we're giving you

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.150
is unclear in some ways.

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:18.280
So yes, absolutely.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:19.310
They knew there were things

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.500
that they could not anticipate.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.030
The Framers themselves
weren't in agreement

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.030
on what freedom of speech means.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:28.830
Some of them enacted,
voted for and got into,

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:32.220
got enacted laws that restricted speech

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.870
in ways that we would
find intolerable today.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.140
We'd say they violate the First Amendment,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:38.550
but here you had some of the guys

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.040
who drafted the First Amendment
voting for those laws.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.270
- [Kim] So near the end of Article Five,

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.330
there's this kind of
long-winded clause that says,

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:51.787
no amendment which may be
made prior to the year 1808

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:58.079
shall in any manner affect
the first and fourth clauses

00:10:58.079 --> 00:11:00.710
in the Ninth Section of the first Article.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.390
Now, if I'm cross-referencing
this correctly,

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.420
what they're really saying here is,

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:08.640
you can't make any
amendments about slavery.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:10.163
So why is this here

00:11:10.163 --> 00:11:13.470
and why are they talking
around it so obliquely?

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.560
- [Rappaport] The
interesting thing about this

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:15.393
is what did they do?

00:11:15.393 --> 00:11:17.663
They basically said, for 20 years,

00:11:17.663 --> 00:11:20.370
there's not gonna be any amendments

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.270
that are going to speak
to the slave trade.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:28.270
And the Constitution is very,
let us say, shy about using

00:11:28.659 --> 00:11:33.659
the term slavery or referring to slavery.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:38.210
It actually never actually
refers to the term slave.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.330
There's a variety of thoughts
about what was going on,

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.860
but one very common view about this

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.170
is that the Constitution was sort

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.630
of a little but embarrassed.

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:50.463
That the Framers were a
little bit embarrassed,

00:11:50.463 --> 00:11:53.290
or at least some of the Framers
were embarrassed about it.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:55.240
And so they didn't wanna make reference

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:56.920
to it too explicitly.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.460
- [Strauss] They might've
been a little bit worried

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:01.400
about what the verdict
of history would be.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.960
So they knew on some level the
sort of demorality of slavery

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.710
but there it is right
there in Article Five

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.780
and there are other places
in the Constitution too

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:11.680
where they don't use the word,

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.697
but what they're doing
is protecting slavery.

00:12:13.697 --> 00:12:16.311
Now they did add these
amendments to the Constitution

00:12:16.311 --> 00:12:18.675
about slavery and about, as you say,

00:12:18.675 --> 00:12:21.358
equal citizenship and voting.

00:12:21.358 --> 00:12:25.280
But the 14th Amendment
providing equal citizenship,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.710
that was pretty much
nullified in most respects

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.880
for a large part of our history.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:33.360
States found a way to get around that.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.290
The 15th Amendment was also something

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.290
that was just not very effective

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:40.990
in preventing African Americans
from being denied the vote.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:44.170
And yes, there are provisions
in the Constitution

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:45.673
that are there and you can evoke them

00:12:45.673 --> 00:12:47.770
and you can rely on them,

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:50.160
but if you just look at the
text of the Constitution,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.880
I think you'd get a misleading impression

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:54.040
about how the Constitution in

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:55.720
our history has actually worked.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.890
- [Kim] Now this is a very good point

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.480
because I think one of the
hardest things for students

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.990
of U.S. history to understand is,

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:06.810
how is it possible that
after the 14th Amendment

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:10.349
was passed, things like Jim Crow happened.

00:13:10.349 --> 00:13:12.183
And I guess the answer is,

00:13:12.183 --> 00:13:17.183
the Constitution is only
enforced if it's enforced.

00:13:18.815 --> 00:13:20.520
- [Strauss] Kim, that's exactly right.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:21.760
It's words on a page.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:23.620
You know that the text is fine.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:25.370
It can say all the right things,

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:27.480
but the institutions and the popular will

00:13:28.398 --> 00:13:32.940
have to be in place to make
something of those fine words.

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:33.773
- [Kim] Interesting.

00:13:33.773 --> 00:13:35.110
So how do you think our
government might be different

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:39.080
if the Constitution didn't
include this amendment process?

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.420
- [Strauss] I don't think it
would've been that different,

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:44.880
just because Article Five gives us such

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.470
a hard process to go through.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:49.920
Just because it's so hard
to amend the Constitution.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.900
We figured out other ways to change

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:54.790
the Constitution in practice,

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.380
even if the words on
the page are the same.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.850
And I think if there were no Article Five,

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.360
we would've found a way to get to where

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.850
we wanted to get to as a country.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:08.410
By those means, by legislation,
by Presidential action,

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.070
by Supreme Court decision

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:12.270
and just by the people
in their lives saying,

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.663
you know, we need to go in this direction.

00:14:15.663 --> 00:14:18.550
We need to go, say, in the
direction of women's equality.

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:19.383
And by the way,

00:14:19.383 --> 00:14:22.270
there's no amendment giving
women equal rights either,

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.830
but that's where we've gotten to

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:25.710
and I think that would've been the pattern

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:28.180
if there were no formal amendment process.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.020
- [Rappaport] There's a second way

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:30.550
in which you could have
Constitutional change,

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.640
which is, you could simply say,

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:36.210
alright, this Constitution
was pretty good for a while.

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:38.160
It's now outlived its usefulness.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.350
Let's have a new Constitution.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.970
That would seem like a very
radical big thing to do.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:46.383
No one, virtually no one proposes
that at the Federal level.

00:14:47.304 --> 00:14:49.500
But in the States, lots of States

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:50.870
have changed their Constitutions.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.880
Not simply passed a
Constitutional Amendment

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.880
but just gotten rid of
the whole old Constitution

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:57.250
and adopted a new one.

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:58.690
That's happened many times.

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:01.920
And so if we didn't have a
Constitutional amendment process,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:03.520
it's quite possible that that's exactly

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:06.790
what we would've seen
at the Federal level.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:07.780
- [Kim] This is really fascinating

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:09.120
because we really think a lot

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:14.120
about what the Framers intended
for certain amendments,

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.970
for example, Freedom of
Speech, Freedom of Religion.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.001
What did they really mean by those things?

00:15:19.001 --> 00:15:22.650
But if we had just kind
of, every now and again,

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:23.940
said, okay we're done with that,

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.720
let's do a new Constitution.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.050
We wouldn't necessarily have that debate.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.828
We'd just say, okay this is
what we meant at the time.

00:15:30.828 --> 00:15:34.330
- [Rappaport] Famously
Thomas Jefferson said oh,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.870
it's really not right
to have a Constitution

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:38.754
that's gonna continue over time

00:15:38.754 --> 00:15:42.790
and bind future generations.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:46.960
And so we aught to have a new
Constitution every 19 years

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.200
when there's a new generation.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.930
And his close friend, James Madison,

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:55.750
had to disagree with
him and basically said,

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:58.370
look I understand why you're saying that,

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:02.260
but you also have to realize
the incredible disruption

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:05.481
that would cause every 20 years.

00:16:05.481 --> 00:16:08.280
People wouldn't be able to
rely on the existing rights

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:09.410
that are in the Constitution

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:13.180
because they would know in
a certain period of time,

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:16.600
new ones would be enacted.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:17.790
We had that debate.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:20.403
Madison won in the sense
that the U.S. Constitution

00:16:20.403 --> 00:16:24.790
is supposed to last
for a long term period.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:27.066
There's no 20 year limit on it.

00:16:27.066 --> 00:16:30.250
And one of the things
that's been beneficial

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.850
for the United States as a result of that,

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.280
is that we've inherited
these Constitutional rights

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.350
that people have a lot of reverence for.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:40.640
- [Kim] So we've learned
that there are two ways

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.370
to amend the Constitution.

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:45.380
Through Congress, or through a special

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:48.450
Constitutional Convention
called by the States.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.040
Either way, adding an
amendment to the Constitution

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.630
is really difficult to do,

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:55.740
so much so that the
American people have only

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.270
in special circumstances used
a Constitutional amendment

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:02.050
to effect social or political change.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:04.000
To learn more about Article Five,

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.940
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:09.100
interactive Constitution
and Khan Academy's resources

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:10.953
on U.S. Government and politics.

